MRI–PET registration quality-check images. They help us see whether the MRI and PET scans of the same person line up correctly before we train your multimodal model.
1. How to understand the images
Each subject has three views, shown side by side.
Axial view — horizontal slice
A cross-section through the head, viewed from above or below. It shows the brain from a horizontal plane.

Coronal view — front-facing slice
Shows the brain as if you are looking at the person face-on, allowing us to compare the left and right sides.

Sagittal view — side slice
Shows the brain from the side, including its internal structures and outer contour.

These are the intended anatomical views. However, your current montage script uses raw image-array indices without first standardizing the NIfTI orientations, so some views may be mislabeled or displayed in an unexpected orientation.
2. What do the grayscale and orange/purple colors mean?
Grayscale image = MRI
MRI shows brain anatomy, including the shape of the brain and surrounding tissues.

Colored overlay = FDG-PET
FDG-PET reflects glucose uptake, which provides information about brain metabolic activity. In this particular color display, brighter orange/yellow generally indicates higher displayed PET signal than darker purple areas.

The colored PET image is overlaid on the grayscale MRI so we can check whether PET activity is located in the corresponding anatomical regions.


Step 4: Prepare the training dataset
This script will create reduced volumes of size 64 × 64 × 48, normalize their intensities, and generate subject-level, stratified train/validation/test splits.
The split will use a fixed random seed so that the same subjects remain assigned to the same set when you rerun the split code.

